  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "violet",
      "title": "Why a side table and not a column pair",
      "items": [
        "Each translatable table has a child keyed unique on (parent_id, locale)",
        "A check constraint pins locale to 'lo' or 'en', so a typo cannot create a third language",
        "Adding a real third language is an INSERT — title_lo / title_en columns would need a migration"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "The slug stays on the parent",
      "items": [
        "One item has one slug, so /events/x and /en/events/x are the same event",
        "A link shared in Lao still opens in English for a reader who switched",
        "Lao is the base: an English row is optional, and its absence is shown, not hidden"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Also translated, also jsonb",
      "items": [
        "event_speakers carries its own translations for talk_title and abstract",
        "Article content, event descriptions and speaker bios are TipTap documents in jsonb",
        "They are sanitised and rendered to HTML server-side, never trusted from the client"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Constraints doing real work",
      "items": [
        "unique (event_id, lower(email)) stops one address registering twice, and lets NULLs through",
        "unique registration_id on event_feedback is the one-response-per-ticket guarantee",
        "events checks end_at > start_at and capacity >= 0, so a broken event cannot be saved"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
